## Michael's Memo: Understanding the Rhythm Behind the Revenue

Not every business runs on a straight line.

Some of the best operators we've had the privilege of working with run in cycles: big peaks, challenging valleys, and a rhythm of revenue that only makes sense when you understand the full picture.

This month's client is exactly that kind of business.

We recently closed an $800,000 term loan underwritten on a revenue-based, historical cash flow model for a merchandise and custom printing company, with a unique twist. The business is built around election cycles. In a presidential election year like 2024, revenue runs north of $30 million. In the post-election year, it drops to about $5 million. It's not a distressed business. It's an extremely cyclical one.

The sharp-revenue-drop story alone gives many lenders pause. Yet there was a second hurdle: the business hadn't filed taxes in five years. Compiled financials existed for every year. There was a legitimate explanation. But nothing had formally been submitted to the IRS. For the vast majority of lenders, that's a fast rejection.

Here's how we worked to overcome it. We supported the owner as he engaged a tax professional experienced in IRS negotiations, and we obtained that engagement in writing. The owner signed IRS authorization forms so our lending partner could verify the tax situation directly rather than taking anyone's word for it. We put the right people on a call together, walked through the situation honestly, and let the documentation do the talking.

Our lender came back with the full $800,000, the owner's complete ask. The deal closed in under three weeks from first conversation to funding.

Not every deal that looks disqualified actually is. Sometimes the path forward just requires a different kind of work: the right lender, the right documentation strategy, and someone willing to advocate for the full story.

If your business, or one you advise, is in an unusual situation, we'd welcome the conversation.

Here to serve,

Michael Kodinsky, Founder & CEO

## Deal Highlight: $800K Bridge Loan for a Commercial Printing and Promotional Merchandise Company

**Client profile**

- Commercial printing and branded merchandise manufacturer
- Revenue tied to election-cycle demand, tens of millions in peak years
- Recently completed two strategic acquisitions, expanding headcount, equipment, and capacity
- Three operating entities under one ownership structure
- Came to Serve Funding during an off-cycle revenue period

**The solution**

- Product: Revenue-Based Term Loan
- Amount: $800,000
- Use of proceeds: fund operations through the off-season, support integration of the acquired entities, and bridge the timing gap ahead of the next election-cycle demand surge

{% callout type="tip" title="A competitive process" %}
The initial offer came in around $494K. Our preferred lending partner leaned in, understood the consolidated picture, and came back with the full $800,000.
{% /callout %}

## More Than Money: Core Value of the Month, Understanding

The "U" in T.R.U.S.T. is Understanding, and this deal is a masterclass in why it matters.

A bank statement in the trough of a cyclical business looks like a problem. Understanding means looking past the snapshot to the full picture: the acquisition strategy, the election-cycle demand pattern, the consolidated cash flows, the trajectory.

Without Understanding, this was a pass. With it, it was an $800,000 funded deal.

That same lens is what we bring to every business we work with:

- We take the time to understand the business, not just the file.
- We build context so the right lenders can see what we see.
- We protect the company's long-term story, not just the near-term transaction.
- We keep everyone around the business informed throughout, because those relationships are what matter most.

Understanding isn't just a value. It's the thing that turns a "no" into a funded deal for a client who deserved capital all along.
